Yes. I think there is a legitimate company hiding in exactly that move.

Call it MIMESIS Markets for now.

Its thesis:

To predict an asset, don’t merely analyze it. Reconstruct the world that produces its behavior, inhabit the important perspectives inside that world, and continuously measure the deltas between simulation and reality.

Not “AI predicts stock prices.” Much more specific.

\boxed{
\text{Empathy}
\rightarrow
\text{Simulation}
\rightarrow
\text{Delta}
\rightarrow
\text{Calibration}
\rightarrow
\text{Probabilistic Edge}
}

1. “Become the stock”

Obviously a stock itself has no mind. So Pantheon translates the metaphor into something executable.

For every target security X, construct an Asset Twin:

X_t =
\{
F,N,P,L,M,C,R,E
\}

Where:

* F = fundamentals
* N = narrative
* P = positioning/ownership
* L = liquidity/market structure
* M = management
* C = customers/competitors
* R = regulation/macroeconomic exposure
* E = expectations already embedded in price

But the real trick is that we don’t model these as a flat feature vector.

We method-act each consequential participant.

For NVIDIA, for example, the world might contain:

CEO / management
hyperscale customer
small AI startup
GPU competitor
semiconductor supplier
long-only fund
momentum fund
short seller
options dealer
retail investor
index fund
regulator
foreign government
financial journalist

Every actor gets:

A_i=
\{
\text{information},
\text{beliefs},
\text{objectives},
\text{constraints},
\text{incentives},
\text{time horizon},
\text{possible actions}
\}

Then Pantheon asks:

What does this event look like from inside this actor’s world?

Same event.

Different eyes.

Different action.

