5. The self-polishing loop

This is where your earlier architecture becomes commercially nasty—in the good sense.

Each prediction is stored before reality occurs:

\hat{Y}_t

Then reality arrives:

Y_t

Compute:

\Delta_t=Y_t-\hat{Y}_t

But also store why we predicted it.

So the ledger contains:

Prediction
Probability
Time horizon
World model used
Actors involved
Expected causal chain
Expected narrative response
Expected positioning response
Actual result
Delta
Which assumptions survived?
Which broke?
What hidden variable appeared?
What should the twin learn?

Over thousands of events:

\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{outcome}
\rightarrow
\text{delta}
\rightarrow
\text{model revision}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}

That’s Pantheon’s Self-Polishing Memory attached to a measurable external scoreboard.

Now we actually know whether we’re getting better.

